exports.description:
RunWithTimeout executes multiple functions concurrently with a timeout.
This function is useful for running cleanup operations, shutdown procedures,
or any set of functions that need to complete within a specified time limit.

The function starts all functions in separate goroutines and waits for them
to complete. If the context times out before all functions finish, the
function returns false. If all functions complete before the timeout,
it returns true.

Key Features:
  - Concurrent execution of multiple functions
  - Context-based timeout support
  - Boolean return value indicating completion status
  - Automatic cleanup when timeout occurs

Parameters:
  - ctx: Context for controlling the timeout
  - fn: Slice of functions to execute concurrently

Returns:
  - bool: true if all functions completed before timeout, false otherwise

Thread Safety:

	This function is thread-safe and can be called concurrently.

Example usage:

	// Define cleanup functions
	cleanupFns := []func(){
		func() { fmt.Println("Cleaning up database connections") },
		func() { fmt.Println("Closing file handles") },
		func() { fmt.Println("Stopping background workers") },
	}

	// Run cleanup with 30-second timeout
	ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 30*time.Second)
	defer cancel()

	completed := RunWithTimeout(ctx, cleanupFns)
	if completed {
		fmt.Println("All cleanup operations completed successfully")
	} else {
		fmt.Println("Cleanup operations timed out")
	}

Example with shorter timeout:

	// Run with a very short timeout to demonstrate timeout behavior
	ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 1*time.Millisecond)
	defer cancel()

	completed := RunWithTimeout(ctx, cleanupFns)
	if !completed {
		fmt.Println("Operations timed out as expected")
	}

